# Away-Watch Idle Tracking — measure the dead clock to size the queue

[SPEC:CXC-IDLE-TRACKING] Track idle time after an away-build completes until David returns
- domain: PLT.CUX
- status: draft — David 2026-07-22 (live), spec-first per [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE]'s lesson
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-RETRO]
- pinned-to: [DEC-IDLE-TRACKING]
- premise: David — "we could have put more in scope for the away session based on the amount you were idling.
  Track how long you are idling after the away build session is complete and until I return — that will help
  us size the queue much more accurately." The 2026-07-22 PM away watch dispatched its whole queue early, then
  the Architect HELD for hours (context reached only ~51%). That dead clock is the sizing signal: idle-minutes
  ÷ deliverable-minutes tells the next run how much MORE to load.

### [CXC-IT-MEASURE] What is measured — three timestamps + one derivation
- `build_complete_ts` — when the away-build QUEUE GOES DRY: the last planned deliverable is committed and the
  Architect enters HOLDING (no further dispatches/commits pending). This is the boundary between productive and
  idle. Stamped by the Architect when it transitions to holding (an explicit `mark_build_complete`), NOT guessed.
- `resumed_ts` — when DAVID RETURNS: the first inbound human channel message after `build_complete_ts`. Stamped
  automatically (the channel bridge sees inbound; see [CXC-IT-STAMP]), so it needs no Architect action.
- `idle_seconds` — DERIVED: `resumed_ts - build_complete_ts` (>= 0; NULL until both ends exist). Never stored
  redundantly — start/end is the single source of truth (mirrors away_watch.elapsed).
- carried alongside the existing away_watch productivity fields (commits, decisions, tokens, tasks) so the ratio
  "idled N min for M deliverables" is one row.

### [CXC-IT-STORE] Where — additive columns on the away_watch table (no new table)
- The `away_watch` row IS the away-session record; idle is a property of that session. Add ADDITIVELY (the
  discipline of the four columns added 2026-07-22): `build_complete_ts REAL`, `resumed_ts REAL`. `idle_seconds`
  is DERIVED in `list_away_watches()` (like `elapsed`), never a stored column.
- `record_away_watch(...)` gains `build_complete_ts` / `resumed_ts` kwargs (default None), upsert-preserving.
- New helpers: `mark_build_complete(label, ts)` (stamp when holding begins) and `mark_resumed(label, ts)` (stamp
  the return; idempotent — only sets if unset AND build_complete_ts is set, so a mid-build message doesn't count).

### [CXC-IT-STAMP] How the two ends are stamped — no guessing, minimal Architect burden
- BUILD-COMPLETE: the Architect calls `mark_build_complete(label, now)` at the exact moment it finishes the
  planned queue and enters holding — the same moment it would post a "planned work complete" checkpoint. One call.
- RESUMED: a deterministic hook on the inbound channel. The channel bridge (or a tiny retrospective consumer)
  watches inbound human messages; the FIRST one after any away_watch has a `build_complete_ts` but no
  `resumed_ts` calls `mark_resumed(label, now)`. Automatic — the Architect never has to notice "David is back."
  (Guard: only human `from=david` inbound, not harness heartbeats or the Architect's own replies.)

### [CXC-IT-SURFACE] How it's surfaced — the sizing readout
- The retrospective + the Away-Watch/Night-Watch view show, per completed away session: `idled H:MM after N
  deliverables in a W-min window` and a utilization ratio `productive / (productive + idle)`. A run that idled
  50% of its window says: next queue ~2x. This is the empirical replacement for the Architect's guess.
- Published on the existing `self/away_watches` projection (away_watch_publisher enriches each watch with the
  derived `idle_seconds` + `idle_fmt`), so the widget renders it with no new namespace.

### [CXC-IT-OPEN] Open / glass
- Auto-detecting build-complete without an explicit Architect call (e.g. N minutes of no commit + no live
  subagent) is a possible enhancement — but explicit-stamp is more honest (the Architect KNOWS when its queue is
  dry) and avoids a false "idle" during a long single dispatch. Explicit for now; auto is a later option.
- Whether to also track idle DURING a run (gaps between dispatches while waiting on subagents) — a finer signal,
  deferred; the after-completion idle is the dominant sizing lever David named.
